# Cursor AI Optimization Guide

## Using Web Context (@web)

Cursor can fetch and reason about live web pages. Use this to save context:

### External API Documentation

Instead of explaining APIs, use `@web`:

```
@web https://developers.docusign.com/docs/esign-rest-api/reference/
@web https://www.attomdata.com/documentation/
@web https://docs.bridgedataoutput.com/
```

Cursor will fetch the actual documentation and understand the API contracts.

### Your Own Website

For landing page or UI work:

```
@web https://silverkey.com
@web http://localhost:5173  (if dev server is running)
```

Paste screenshots or HTML directly into Composer to match/extend designs.

### Public Examples

Reference similar implementations:

```
@web https://github.com/similar-project/feature-you-want
```

## MCP Servers (Model Context Protocol)

Cursor supports MCP for connecting to live data sources. This makes Cursor **see** rather than **guess**.

### Recommended MCP Servers for SilverKey

**1. mcp-server-postgres**
- Point at your dev PostgreSQL database
- Cursor can inspect real schema, relationships, and data
- Eliminates schema guessing errors

**Installation:**
```bash
npm install -g @modelcontextprotocol/server-postgres
```

**Configuration (in Cursor settings):**
```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "postgres": {
      "command": "mcp-server-postgres",
      "args": ["postgresql://user:pass@localhost:5432/silverkey_dev"]
    }
  }
}
```

**2. mcp-server-filesystem**
- Structured file access with better context
- Faster than reading files one-by-one

**3. mcp-server-aws** (community)
- Query AWS resources directly
- Inspect S3 buckets, Cognito pools, RDS instances
- Verify resource names and configurations

### Benefits

With MCP servers, Cursor can:
- Read your **actual** database schema instead of guessing column names
- See **real** user preferences data structure
- Verify AWS resource names before generating code
- Understand relationships between tables automatically

## Linter Integration

Cursor reads linter diagnostics natively and will fix errors when asked.

### Python (ruff + mypy)

Configured in `Server/pyproject.toml`:
- Ruff catches style, imports, and common bugs
- Mypy adds gradual type checking
- Cursor sees errors in real-time and can fix them

Run manually:
```bash
cd Server
ruff check . --fix
mypy .
```

### TypeScript (ESLint + Prettier)

Configured in `Client/packages/config/eslint/`:
- ESLint enforces architecture rules (custom silverkey plugins)
- Prettier handles formatting
- Cursor sees errors in real-time

Run manually:
```bash
cd Client
npm run lint
npm run format
```

### Pre-commit Hooks

Install once:
```bash
pip install pre-commit
pre-commit install
```

Now every commit:
1. Runs ruff, mypy, eslint automatically
2. Fails if linters find issues
3. Cursor sees the error output and self-corrects

This creates a **feedback loop**: Cursor → Edit → Lint Fails → Cursor Sees Error → Fixes → Success

## Tips for Better Context

### 1. Reference Architecture Files

At session start for complex tasks:
```
@ARCHITECTURE.md
@Client/ARCHITECTURE.md
@documentation/server/flask-architecture.md
```

### 2. Use Specific @-references

Instead of:
```
"Fix the user preferences bug"
```

Better:
```
@Server/app/services/aggregation/preferences_aggregation_write.py
@.cursor/rules/shared/user-preferences-schema.mdc
"Fix the user preferences bug - see schema contract in rules"
```

### 3. Leverage .cursorignore

Already configured to exclude:
- Build artifacts (dist/, coverage/)
- Dependencies (node_modules/, .venv/)
- Migrations (Server/migrations/versions/)
- Generated files

Cursor won't waste tokens indexing these.

### 4. Use Rules Files

Rules in `.cursor/rules/` are **automatically applied** to every session.
- Backend patterns: `.cursor/rules/backend/`
- Frontend patterns: `.cursor/rules/frontend/`
- Shared conventions: `.cursor/rules/shared/`

No need to explain conventions every session.

## Performance Tips

### Parallel Exploration

When starting a new feature, launch multiple explore agents:
```
One agent: Search for similar patterns in codebase
Another agent: Read API documentation
Third agent: Analyze database schema
```

### Composer vs Chat

- **Composer**: Multi-file refactors, cross-cutting changes
- **Chat**: Single-file questions, quick edits

### Session Hygiene

- Start new Composer session for unrelated tasks
- Don't let context accumulate across different features
- Pin key files with @ at session start rather than letting Cursor rediscover

## Troubleshooting

### "Cursor keeps guessing wrong field names"

→ Use MCP postgres server to let it see real schema

### "Cursor doesn't follow our patterns"

→ Make rules more specific in `.cursor/rules/` (see constraints section)

### "Linter errors after Cursor edits"

→ Install pre-commit hooks; Cursor will see failures and self-correct

### "Context window filling up"

→ Check `.cursorignore` excludes build artifacts and dependencies

